  simply appear in the panel with no toast, and the badge count tells you they're there.

  [1mData flow[0m, one direction:

  engine tick (60s)
    -> rules.js(tasks, ledger, now)      pure
    -> new DueReminder[]
    -> ledger.record()  +  notifications.add()
    -> store emits 'change'
    -> badge re-renders, panel re-renders, toast shown for this-tick items only

  Task mutations feed the same loop: editing a due date clears that task's ledger entries, so the next tick re-evaluates
  it from scratch. Completing or deleting a task dismisses its unread notifications — an overdue nag for something you
  already finished is the fastest way to make a notification system feel broken.

  The 60-second tick is deliberately coarse. Reminder lead times are in minutes; a sub-minute tick buys nothing and
  burns battery. The tick is also injectable, so tests drive it directly.

  [1mOne thing I've left out:[0m notification grouping. Ten tasks going overdue at once produces ten entries and ten toasts.
  Real systems collapse that. I'd rather ship ungrouped and see if it actually bites, since grouping is
  presentation-layer only and adds no data-model debt.
